\
The Cognitive OS owns shared state.\
\
Environments consume and transform that state according to their defined responsibilities.\
\
3. Existing Twelve Environments\
\
Preserve the existing environment architecture.\
\
Environment	Primary function\
Laboratory	Verification & Evidence\
Library	Retrieval-Augmented Scholarship\
Workshop	Constraint-Driven Construction\
Forest	High-Temperature Associative Exploration\
Observatory	Global Pattern Abstraction\
Attic	Low-Confidence Reactivation\
Cellar	Assumption-Challenging / Dialectical\
Garden	Cross-Domain Synthesis\
Arena	Adversarial Stress-Testing\
Cloister	Contemplative Dwelling\
Archive	Longitudinal Trajectory Reasoning\
Threshold	Compression & Final Commitment\
\
Do not collapse these environments into generic agent roles.\
\
Their environmental distinctions should remain meaningful.\
\
4. Cognitive OS Services\
\
Implement six first-class services.\
\
4.1 Belief Revision + Truth Maintenance Service\
\
Responsibilities:\
\
Belief Revision\
\
Manage transitions between:\
\
asserted\
defeasible\
under_challenge\
retracted\
reinstated\
superseded\
stale\
Truth Maintenance\
\
Maintain dependencies between:\
\
evidence\
claims\
assumptions\
derived claims\
decisions\
actions\
\
Example:\
\
Evidence A\
   \uc0\u8595 \
Claim B\
   \uc0\u8595 \
Claim C\
   \uc0\u8595 \
Decision D\
\
If Evidence A becomes invalid:\
\
A invalidated\
   \uc0\u8595 \
B becomes stale/unsupported\
   \uc0\u8595 \
C requires review\
   \uc0\u8595 \
D becomes review-required\
\
The system must not silently retain downstream conclusions whose dependencies have been invalidated.\
\
Requirements\
\
Implement:\
\
claim registry\
dependency graph\
revision events\
retraction propagation\
reinstatement conditions\
stale-state detection\
dependency traversal\
revision history\
5. Knowledge State / Dynamic Epistemic Service\
\
Do not implement full formal Dynamic Epistemic Logic unless required.\
\
Implement its useful computational primitives.\
\
The system must represent:\
\
what the system currently knows\
what it believes\
what it does not know\
what has changed\
why it changed\
what was communicated\
what was committed\
\
Define explicit event types:\
\
OBSERVE\
RETRIEVE\
INFER\
COMMUNICATE\
CORRECT\
CHALLENGE\
RETRACT\
REINSTATE\
COMMIT\
EXECUTE\
REVIEW\
\
Events must be timestamped and versioned.\
\
A knowledge transition should be represented as an event, not merely as a modified field.\
\
6. Event Store / Temporal State\
\
Introduce an immutable event log.\
\
Every meaningful epistemic or decision transition should produce an event.\
\
Example:\
\
\{\
  "event_id": "uuid",\
  "event_type": "RETRACT",\
  "timestamp": "ISO8601",\
  "actor": "Laboratory",\
  "claim_id": "uuid",\
  "previous_state_version": 12,\
  "new_state_version": 13,\
  "reason": "New evidence invalidated source",\
  "causal_dependencies": ["event-id-1", "event-id-7"]\
\}\
\
Current state should be derivable from the event history.\
\
Do not make historical state mutable.\
\
Archive should be able to reconstruct:\
\
What did the system believe?\
When did it believe it?\
Why did it believe it?\
What changed?\
What caused the change?\
What decisions depended upon it?\
