26. Important Architectural Constraints\
Constraint 1 \'97 No silent belief mutation\
\
Any meaningful belief change must generate an event.\
\
Constraint 2 \'97 No destruction of history\
\
Never overwrite epistemic history.\
\
Create a new version.\
\
Constraint 3 \'97 No identity-based truth\
\
Identity coherence cannot establish factual truth.\
\
Constraint 4 \'97 No score-only reasoning\
\
Scores are summaries.\
\
Underlying claims, evidence, dependencies and events remain authoritative.\
\
Constraint 5 \'97 No unrestricted agent access\
\
Every environment has explicit read/write permissions.\
\
Constraint 6 \'97 No automatic reinstatement\
\
Attic may surface an old belief.\
\
Laboratory/Cellar must establish whether it should return.\
\
Constraint 7 \'97 No automatic commitment\
\
Only Threshold can commit a consequential decision.\
\
Constraint 8 \'97 No philosophical black boxes\
\
Every theoretical concept must become an explicit computational primitive.\
\
Constraint 9 \'97 Preserve uncertainty\
\
Compression must not erase uncertainty, provenance or unresolved objections.\
\
Constraint 10 \'97 Everything consequential must be traceable\
\
For every decision the system should be able to answer:\
\
What did we believe?\
Why?\
Based on what?\
What challenged it?\
What changed?\
Who/what caused the change?\
What dependencies existed?\
Why was this action finally committed?\
27. Definition of Done\
\
The Cognitive OS foundation is considered operational when the system can demonstrate the following scenario:\
\
Scenario\
\
The system makes a decision based on Claims A, B and C.\
\
Later, new evidence invalidates Claim A.\
\
The system must automatically:\
\
1. Register new evidence.\
2. Create an epistemic event.\
3. Update Claim A.\
4. Increment the belief-state version.\
5. Propagate the change through the TMS.\
6. Identify Claims B/C as affected where appropriate.\
7. Identify the dependent decision.\
8. Increase epistemic debt where unresolved.\
9. Mark the decision as requiring review.\
10. Preserve the original decision.\
11. Route the issue to appropriate environments.\
12. Allow new evidence/arguments to be evaluated.\
13. Re-run adversarial testing if required.\
14. Re-run identity/stance reflection if required.\
15. Produce a new decision version.\
16. Preserve the complete trajectory.\
\
If this works reliably, SageReasoning has acquired a genuine temporal epistemic architecture, rather than merely a collection of reasoning agents.\
\
