One further change I would make to your overall conceptual model\
\
I think the Perplexity/Grok work has uncovered something more significant than simply "six extra services."\
\
Your original architecture was roughly:\
\
reasoning across different cognitive environments.\
\
This new architecture adds:\
\
continuity of the thing doing the reasoning.\
\
That gives you four distinct dimensions:\
\
Dimension	SageReasoning capability\
Spatial	12 cognitive environments\
Epistemic	claims, evidence, uncertainty, revision\
Temporal	versions, events, trajectories, reopening\
Relational	identity, conversation, coupling, multi-agent coordination\
\
And the Stoic harness then sits across those dimensions as a normative decision-integrity layer.\
\
That is substantially more interesting than simply adding more "reasoning agents."\
\
The architectural test I would use from here\
\
Don't ask Claude:\
\
"Can we implement these six theories?"\
\
Ask:\
\
"Can SageReasoning maintain a coherent identity, belief state, and decision history while its beliefs change through evidence, interaction, environmental change and adversarial challenge?"\
\
If the answer eventually becomes yes, you've moved from an agent workflow into something much closer to a cognitive operating system.\
\
And I would make the belief-retraction \uc0\u8594  dependency-propagation \u8594  decision-reopening test the first major milestone. If that works cleanly, almost everything else in this proposal has a proper foundation.}